\section{Cache a Result for One Call Site, Not One Method Name}

A drawing loop sends \texttt{paint} to a sequence of
objects. If the loop usually sees circles, the runtime
can remember the result of looking up \texttt{paint}
on a circle at that call site and test whether it still
applies. The same method name at another call site may
mostly see labels and benefit from a different entry.
H\"olzle, Chambers, and Ungar's SELF work \cite{holzle1991pic} describes the
extension from one cached result to several: a polymorphic
inline cache. The collected receiver types also provided
feedback for recompilation. Profiles characterize
observed calls, not all legal calls.

Consider a small interpreter with single inheritance,
mutable method definitions, and immutable class identity
per receiver. A cache entry records the class, target,
and a dispatch world number. The number changes whenever
any method lookup result can change: replacing an
inherited method must invalidate results on subclasses,
even when their identity and own declarations stay the
same. A coarse counter is easy to explain; a production
runtime may track more precise dependencies for the
method family and classes traversed by lookup.

\section{Specify What Happens as Types Accumulate}

\Cref{fig:visual-407} shows a two-case fast path. A miss
goes to baseline lookup, which may extend the cache or
mark the site megamorphic. Varying receivers must not
change whether an invalid send raises, what target a
valid send selects, or which receiver is passed. The
cache stores an entry only when the baseline returns a
plain callable target under facts represented by its guard.

