\section{Preserve the Operands on All Paths}

\begin{verbatim}
send(site, recv, args):
    require_receiver(recv)
    world := dispatch_world
    if site.world != world: site.reset(world)
    cls := class_of(recv)
    if site.mode != Mega:
        for entry in site.entries:
            if entry.cls == cls:
                return invoke(entry.target, recv, args)

    target := baseline_select(cls, site.method)
    if site.mode != Mega and target.cacheable:
        if length(site.entries) == capacity:
            site.become_mega()
        else:
            site.append(cls, target)
    return invoke(target, recv, args)

replace_method(owner, method, new_body):
    require_dispatch_mutation_boundary()
    owner.methods[method] := new_body
    dispatch_world := fresh_world_number()
\end{verbatim}

This single-executor model permits mutations at dispatch
boundaries, not between lookup or a successful guard and
entry into the selected body. The body itself can perform an
edit that affects subsequent selections. The receiver and
argument expressions have already run. The miss path
receives their values; it must not replay those
expressions. With other threads, reading the counter once
is not a complete protocol. The VM needs coherent
publication, a definition of when a concurrent edit
affects selection, and a way to keep old code alive
while calls can still enter or execute it.

\section{Devirtualize Under a Proof or a Guard}

A known final method can be called directly if the
language and linkage rules prevent replacement. A
closed-world class analysis might also show that every
possible receiver selects the same target. In an open
world, that reasoning must be backed by a dependency
on the absence of relevant new subclasses or methods.
One alternative is an explicit exact-class guard plus
method-validity guard and a normal fallback. Direct
selection can enable inlining, but does not itself
require inlining or deoptimization.

\begin{verbatim}
optimized_send(recv, args):
    if class_of(recv) != Circle or not assumption.valid:
        resume_baseline_at_send(recv, args)
    else:
        inline_circle_paint(recv, args)
\end{verbatim}

If all that happened before the guard was evaluation of
the operands, resuming at the send is legitimate. A guard
inserted inside the inlined body needs a different resume
point, especially after drawing effects. Recovery data
may reconstruct locals, materialize virtual objects,
and recreate missing inlined frames
\cite{fluckiger2022deoptless}. Such reconstruction
describes interpreter state; it does not replace the
machine root maps used to find live references at
collection points (\Cref{ch:ext-411}). If a collection
occurs during the miss, reload moved operands through
updated root locations before invoking.

\section{Exercise Edits With and Without Active Optimized Code}

Invalidating future entries and revoking assumptions in
already executing code are separate tasks. At a
safepoint, a runtime can mark dependent optimized entries
unusable for new calls and arrange to leave affected
active frames before they execute code relying on
invalid facts. Define what happens to a method invocation
that has already selected an old body. For example,
the language may let that invocation finish while
requiring its next send to use current methods. A cache
reset alone cannot recover an inlined send that no longer
consults the cache.

Test a loop that logs argument evaluation once per send.
Warm up on circles, add a label to exercise the new
receiver path, then edit an inherited circle implementation
without changing the circle's identity. Compare returned
values and emitted events to baseline. Trigger a guard
failure after an observable drawing command in an inlined
frame and ensure the resume point neither draws it twice
nor skips later work. Finally let an unrelated site
remain hot during the edit: with precise dependencies it
should stay optimized, whereas the coarse-world model
can legitimately discard its cache. Both policies must
agree on results, even when they disagree on cost.

\section{Record Why the Cached Lookup Reached That Body}

Imagine \texttt{Circle} inherits \texttt{paint} from
\texttt{Shape}. A precise dependency is not simply
``the body on Shape is unchanged''. It includes the
absence of a more specific definition on every step up
to that owner. Installing \texttt{Circle.paint} changes
the first match without touching \texttt{Shape}. Moving
a prototype link in a prototype-based interpreter can
have a similar effect. Cache dependencies have to match
the premises of the actual lookup rule.

\begin{verbatim}
select Circle.paint:
    Circle.paint absent      record absence at Circle
    Shape.paint present     record target at Shape
edit Circle.paint:
    invalidate absence at Circle, keep other Shape users
\end{verbatim}

A dependency index can store cached and compiled artifacts
against both kinds of fact. Under a stop-the-world editing
protocol, install the new methods and invalidate affected
artifacts before resuming threads; a background compiler
must also revalidate assumptions before publishing code
compiled from an older snapshot. Finally, test that an
invalidated entry is still retained as long as some paused
frame needs its code or reconstruction metadata. Selection
validity and storage lifetime are independent.
